<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hephaestus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hḗphaistos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hephaestus is the Greek god of artisans, blacksmiths, craftsmen, fire, metalworking, sculpture and volcanoes. In Greek mythology he was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, either on her own, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a>, or by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and he was cast off Mount Olympus because of his lameness, by Hera in one account and by Zeus in another. As the blacksmith of the gods he made all their weapons, and his symbols are the smith's hammer, the anvil and a pair of tongs. His cult was based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, and he was worshipped in the manufacturing centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> name found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> probably attests his worship. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he was equated with Vulcan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hephaestus.jpg" alt="Hephaestus" width="330" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Foundry Painter (name vase) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hephaistos_Thetis_at_Kylix_by_the_Foundry_Painter_Antikensammlung_Berlin_F2294.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
